A||N||2||2xsess||E-Brake Cable replacement||||5||
Z||000000||2xsess||09-10-2000||06:19 PM||||Has anyone ever replaced all the e-brake cables on their Fiero? I was wondering how large of a task it is? I haven't bought a manual yet. Thanks for your input.||||reg||5
Z||000001||AL||09-10-2000||09:05 PM||||it's not that hard, first jack the car up remove the tires,then get underneath and with a wrench 8mm or 9mm or10mm loosen the 2 cables at the drivers side where they meet,once you've done that then go to each tire and with a screw driver remove the springs on each side and then unhook the wires from the calipers,then go 2 the drivers side wheel well and take the screws off to remove the wheel well cover,then go inside and remove the speaker cover and bottom trim and every thing will be pretty basic.once you've done all that just do everything in reverse to install.if you think it's to hard grab your video cam and have someone record everthing and that way you can go to the video to see if you are doing it right.good luck and go slow and you will do it. right.<B>AL</B> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/AL_sig.gif">  <P>------------------<BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||William Federle||09-10-2000||09:09 PM||||I just replaced the left and right rear cables on my 1984 Fiero. I had to replace the calipers (one because it started leaking after I replaced the pads - the other because I wanted to make the E brake work and the lever was very difficult to move). The most difficult thing I've done so far is install the return springs - the easiest way is to put the bottom of the spring where it goes, and then to bend the top over with a very large pair of channel locks to get it under the lever. I've ordered the front cable and I think I'll have a working ebrake when I get that installed.<P>Taking the old cables out just requires a little work - I rotated them and when I'd get to a retaining finger on the retaining fitting, I'd press it in with a screw-driver.<P>||||reg||1
